<section xml:id="sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals">
  <title>Exponents and radicals</title>

  <introduction><p>Rewriting roots and reciprocals as powers can simplify differentiation and integration. The exponent laws must be used on domains where the expressions are defined.</p></introduction><subsection xml:id="appsubsec-laws-exponents">
    <title>Laws of exponents</title>

  <p>For positive bases, the usual exponent laws are:</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals-0">
    <title>Laws of exponents</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><alert>law</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>example</alert></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle a^m a^n=a^{m+n}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle x^2x^5=x^7</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac{x^7}{x^3}=x^4</m>, <m>x\neq0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle (a^m)^n=a^{mn}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle (x^2)^5=x^{10}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle (ab)^n=a^n b^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle (3x)^2=9x^2</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \left(\frac ab\right)^n=\frac{a^n}{b^n}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^3=\frac{x^3}{8}</m>, <m>b\neq0</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>The following two inequalities illustrate common traps; they hold in general rather than for every possible choice of inputs:</p>

  <p><me>(a+b)^2\neq a^2+b^2</me></p>

  <p>and</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{a+b}\neq \sqrt a+\sqrt b.</me></p>

  <p>For example,</p>

  <p><me>(2+3)^2=25,</me></p>

  <p>but</p>

  <p><me>2^2+3^2=13.</me></p>

  <p>Also,</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{9+16}=5,</me></p>

  <p>but</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt9+\sqrt{16}=7.</me></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-negative-fractional-exponents">
    <title>Negative and fractional exponents</title>

  <p>A negative exponent means reciprocal:</p>

  <p><me>
a^{-n}=\frac1{a^n},
\qquad a\neq0.
</me></p>

  <p>For an integer <m>n&gt;0</m>, a fractional exponent means a root:</p>

  <p><me>
a^{1/n}=\sqrt[n]{a}
</me></p>

  <p>when the root is defined. For a negative base, write a rational exponent <m>m/n</m> in lowest terms with <m>n&gt;0</m>; a real value requires <m>n</m> odd. If <m>m&lt;0</m>, also exclude a zero base. More generally,</p>

  <p><me>
a^{m/n}=\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^m.
</me></p>

  <p>For real-valued functions, even roots require nonnegative inputs:</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{x}</me></p>

  <p>is real only for</p>

  <p><me>x\ge0.</me></p>

  <p>Odd roots allow all real inputs:</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt[3]{x}</me></p>

  <p>is real for every real <m>x</m>.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals-1">
    <title>Negative and fractional exponents</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="33.3%"/><col width="33.3%"/><col width="33.4%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>expression</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>power form</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>domain as a real function</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>\sqrt{x}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x^{1/2}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x\ge0</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>\sqrt[3]{x}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x^{1/3}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>all real <m>x</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle \frac1{x^2}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x^{-2}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x\neq0</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle \frac1{\sqrt{x}}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x^{-1/2}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>x&gt;0</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><m>\sqrt{x^2+1}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>(x^2+1)^{1/2}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>all real <m>x</m></p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals-1">
    <title>Example</title>
    <p>Rewrite using powers:</p>

  <p><me>\frac{3}{x^2\sqrt{x}}.</me></p>

  <p>Since</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2},</me></p>

  <p>we have</p>

  <p><me>x^2\sqrt{x}=x^2x^{1/2}=x^{5/2}.</me></p>

  <p>Therefore</p>

  <p><me>
\frac{3}{x^2\sqrt{x}}=3x^{-5/2},
\qquad x&gt;0.
</me></p>

  <p>This form is easier to differentiate or integrate.</p>
    </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals-2">
    <title>Example</title>
    <p>Simplify</p>

  <p><me>(x^{3/2})(x^{5/2}).</me></p>

  <p>Add exponents:</p>

  <p><me>x^{3/2}x^{5/2}=x^{(3/2+5/2)}=x^{8/2}=x^4.</me></p>

  <p>So</p>

  <p><me>
(x^{3/2})(x^{5/2})=x^4.
</me></p>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-radicals-conjugates">
    <title>Radicals and conjugates</title>

  <p>The conjugate of</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{A}+\sqrt{B}</me></p>

  <p>is</p>

  <p><me>\sqrt{A}-\sqrt{B}.</me></p>

  <p>Multiplying gives</p>

  <p><me>(\sqrt{A}+\sqrt{B})(\sqrt{A}-\sqrt{B})=A-B.</me></p>

  <example xml:id="ex-sec-a-3-exponents-and-radicals-3">
    <title>Example</title>
    <p>Simplify</p>

  <p><me>\frac{1}{\sqrt{x+4}-2}.</me></p>

  <p>Multiply by the conjugate:</p>

  <p><me>\frac{1}{\sqrt{x+4}-2}
\cdot
\frac{\sqrt{x+4}+2}{\sqrt{x+4}+2}.</me></p>

  <p>The denominator becomes</p>

  <p><me>(x+4)-4=x.</me></p>

  <p>Therefore</p>

  <p><me>
\frac{1}{\sqrt{x+4}-2}
=
\frac{\sqrt{x+4}+2}{x},
</me></p>

  <p>where the original expression requires</p>

  <p><me>x\ge -4
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
x\neq0.</me></p>

  <p>The condition <m>x\neq0</m> appears because the original denominator is <m>0</m> when
<m>\sqrt{x+4}=2</m>, which happens at <m>x=0</m>.</p>
    </example>
  </subsection>

</section>
